                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Daedalus: No Escape is a fast little top down shooter that takes a lot of ideas from old arena shooters and puts them into a 2D space. I play as a space marine running around dark sci fi arenas, picking up weapons and trying to survive while enemies come at me from every direction. The basic idea is very simple, but I like how quickly it gets into the action. There is no long story or complicated setup. I can start a match and immediately focus on moving, shooting and staying alive.\n\nThe shooting is easily the best part for me. There are nine weapons to find, and having to move around the maps looking for better equipment gives each match a nice sense of momentum. I can use a more FPS style control scheme or a fixed camera setup, which gives me some choice over how I want the game to feel. The movement is quick and the arenas encourage me to keep moving instead of standing in one place. When several enemies are attacking at once, the simple controls work quite well because I can concentrate on positioning and aiming rather than fighting with the interface.\n\nI also like the amount of modes included. I can play high score challenges, deathmatch, last man standing, team deathmatch and capture the flag, so there is more here than one basic arena mode. There are also lots of maps and a level editor, which is something I appreciate in a small game like this. The editor gives me another reason to experiment with the game instead of treating the included maps as the entire experience. There are also 37 achievements, so there is a decent amount of extra stuff for me to work towards.\n\nThe biggest weakness for me is that the game is very much built around its core combat. That combat is fun, but there is not much else supporting it. There is no proper campaign with a developing story, character progression or equipment system that gives me a long term reason to keep improving my marine. The single player side is mainly about playing against bots and chasing high scores, which works for short sessions but does not give me the same feeling of progression that a larger shooter would. I can enjoy the simplicity, but eventually I start wanting something more substantial.\n\nThe age of the game is also noticeable. Daedalus: No Escape was released in 2014, and while the dark sci fi presentation still works, the visuals are fairly basic compared with newer shooters. I actually think the simple style suits the top down perspective, but some maps and effects feel dated. The soundtrack helps quite a bit because it gives the matches more atmosphere and makes the action feel more energetic. I also like that the game supports Windows, Linux and older versions of macOS, although the current Steam version has some operating system limitations that I would need to keep in mind.\n\nWhat I appreciate most is that the game knows exactly what it wants to be. It is not trying to turn a top down shooter into a huge RPG or story driven adventure. It takes the arena shooter formula and changes the perspective while keeping the fast movement, weapon pickups and different competitive modes. The level editor is a particularly good addition because it gives the basic mechanics more room to breathe. I also like that the game is free to play, which makes the limited progression much easier for me to accept.\n\nOverall, Daedalus: No Escape is a fun old school style arena shooter that works best when I want something quick and uncomplicated. I like the fast combat, weapon variety, different modes, map selection and level editor, and the top down perspective gives the familiar arena formula its own identity. The lack of deeper progression and the dated presentation stop it from reaching the higher ratings I give to more complete games, but I think the core shooting is good enough to carry it for quite a while. For me, it is a solid little free shooter that does not need to be more complicated than it is.\n\nRating: 7.5/10\n",
